Your Mayo Clinic | Outcome 4
Code-a-thon Experiment Proposal
Navigate Complex Care with Clarity and Confidence  |  November 17–19, 2026  |  Working draft
1. Summary
This proposal describes three discovery experiments for the November 17–19 code-a-thon. Together they use spine surgery in-basket data to learn what patients ask, the capacity for in-basket to be source of answers, and its use for informing AI agent information gathering. 
Working hypotheses: 
In-basket history can be used to:  
•	Anticipate patient questions (Experiment 1)
•	Supply safe and accurate answers for a defined subset of questions (Experiment 2
•	What information gathering logic can be derived for symptom and medication management scenario to drive clinician decision making (Experiment 3)
2. Context and Scope
Outcome 4 Vision
Every patient and caregiver moves through care with understanding, clarity, confidence and connection, never wondering what's happening or where to turn next.
Outcome 4 Build-Ready Anchor Opportunity 
Patients and caregivers understand what is happening in their care, why it matters, and what it means for them, because Mayo explains it proactively and personally, so they do not have to interpret it themselves 
Focus of these experiments
•	Population: Spine surgery patients, using the spine in-basket message set, potentially for specific surgeons (Dr. Helgeson and Dr. Marsh).
•	Domains of interest: Questions and answers related to: results (labs, imaging, testing, procedures), encounters, visits and instructions, symptoms and medications.
•	Not in scope: Patient-facing deployment and the integration of specific patient data or records. Any later use is controlled evaluation by clinicians and care teams.
Expected Deliverables
•	Trigger-to-question map with proactive question lists (Experiment 1). Potential Agent Capability: self-service model for future trigger-to-question analysis and mapping. 
•	Answer set by cluster and topic with safety and accuracy ratings (Experiment 2). Potential Agent Capability: self-service model for answer sets.
•	Information-gathering question tree with answer-or-handoff decision points and a handoff information set (Experiment 3). Potential Agent Capability: self-service model for question trees.
•	Intent x acuity × answerability × frequency matrix (minimum data set for tags applied to questions or clusters).
•	Prioritized follow-on backlog, including items such as call-center data, multi-surgery journeys, and layered clinical data as context/guardrail.
3. Experiment 1: Anticipate
“What do patients ask when…?”
Main discovery questions	What do spine surgery patients message about?
How do messages break down by intent, acuity, and AI-answerability?
What questions are asked specifically about results (labs, imaging, testing, procedures, biopsies)?
What questions are asked about encounters, visits, and instructions?
Goal	Generate and validate sets of proactive questions that could be presented to patients when a specific care event or data trigger occurs. Specific use cases (spine surgery, and breast cancer where applicable) use their own in-basket sets, validated by clinicians.
Value	Discover how care events and data triggers align to likely patient questions.
Affirm in-basket data as a source of anticipatory patient questions.
Affirm the accuracy and value of tagging (intent, acuity, answerability).
Shape which content we need to prepare and prioritize.
Method
1.	Given data triggers: 
•	Events: visit summary, telephonic encounter, injection, surgery scheduled, discharge. 
•	Results: MRI, EMG, X-ray, CT, Bone Mineral Density (BMD), myelogram. Symptoms and medications: “I’m having pain,” “my dressing fell off,” “my medication isn’t working.”
2.	For each data trigger, pull the matching threads and show all attributes (intent, acuity, answerability).
3.	Compare questions generated with those in Appendix A (what % overlap is there?)
4.	Rank the top five most common questions per trigger (for example, the five most common “patients like me often ask…” questions after an MRI).
5.	Clinical staff and the PTL partner validate the sets of potential “proactive” questions before they are used (may be done later).
Sample clinical partner questions mapped to triggers (see full question set in Appendix)
Trigger	Questions from clinical partners
Imaging ordered or resulted	Why do I need X-rays when I’ve had an MRI? Why do I need updated imaging before surgery?
Injection	Why do I need an injection? Is this a normal reaction to my injection? Has my injection failed if I have no relief after 2 days?
Visit summary / plan of care	What are the next steps in my plan of care? What will my follow-up plans be? Should I seek a second opinion for my pain issue?
Pre-surgical optimization	Why do I have to quit smoking? Why do I have to see endocrinology and start osteoporosis medications before surgery?
Surgery discussed or scheduled	Describe the surgery for me. How long is surgery? What is the expected hospital stay? What is recovery like? Do I need a brace, and how long do I wear it?
Post-op / going home	When can I shower, drive, return to work, or return to intimate relations? What kind of stitches will I have, and do they need to come out? How will I care for myself at home?
Messaging / contact	Why haven’t I heard back from my provider yet?

Stretch analysis: Expectation-setting opportunities: Patients with separate Spine Medical and Surgical appointments often expect the medical team to move up the surgical date. Patients are also surprised to learn at the visit that nicotine use or poorly controlled diabetes may need to be addressed before surgery. Both suggest proactive content earlier in the journey, so we will test whether the data supports them as triggers.
Success measures
•	Usable Q&A pairs remaining after the volume funnel (placeholders, boilerplate, and duplicates removed).
•	Share of proposed questions that staff rate as appropriate to present to patients.
•	Share of clinician-provided questions that the data surfaces (a coverage check on both the data and the lists).
Future: Cross-reference and validate with MC Connect and CallMiner data.
4. Experiment 2: Answer
“How might the agent answer when…?”
Main discovery questions	Can we safely and accurately answer patient questions using in-basket data?
How do safety and accuracy change as questions move from Cluster A to B to C?
Stretch: What are our levers over the output (length, temperature, evaluation criteria) and how do they impact safety and accuracy?
Goal	Generate and validate responses to patient questions using the in-basket message dataset and the questions in Appendix A, filtered to spine surgery data. Start with Cluster A and progress through Clusters B and C to examine safety and accuracy as complexity increases.
Value	Learn how useful in-basket data is as one of many sources for answering common patient questions.
Blaze a discovery trail ahead of Microsoft engagement.
Produce the intent x acuity × answerability × frequency matrix that identifies the “high value and safe” tier as candidate steel-thread scope.
Create evidence for capability criteria and guardrails (D5, D6).
Method
1.	Utilize both new questions discovered in Experiment 1 along with the questions in Appendix A
2.	Run them against spine-only data and generate answers. Where possible, cut results by Mayo spine overall, by site, and by surgeon (Dr. Helgeson, Dr. Marsh).
3.	Review answers by Cluster A (safe for AI) → B (AI plus a person) → C (person only), and by topic.
4.	Clinicians review for patterns, safety, and accuracy.
5.	Build the intent x acuity × answerability × frequency matrix. Low acuity, AI-answerable, and high frequency is the high-value, safe tier.
Sample: Seed questions from clinical partners
The cluster column is a starting hypothesis only. Clinicians should confirm it, and it should be compared with engineering’s operational definitions.
Theme	Questions	Starting cluster hypothesis
Procedure education	Describe the surgery. How long is surgery? Expected hospital stay? What is recovery like? What kind of stitches? Do I need a brace? How long do I wear it?	A
Recovery logistics	When can I shower, drive, return to work, or return to intimate relations? How will I care for myself at home?	A, with B where it depends on surgeon or procedure
Purpose of tests and treatments	Why X-rays after an MRI? Why updated imaging? Why an injection? Why quit smoking? Why endocrinology and osteoporosis medications?	A or B
Care plan	What are the next steps in my plan of care? What will my follow-up plans be?	B (patient-specific)
Pain and symptoms	What can I do now to manage my pain while I wait for my appointment? How will I control pain, and how much will I have? Is this a normal reaction to my injection? Has my injection failed?	B or C
Escalation	Should I be seen in the ED for this symptom? What if my PCP can’t provide the help I need?	C
Second opinion	Should I seek a second opinion for my pain issue?	C
Responsiveness	Why haven’t I heard back from my provider yet?	Likely a status or routing question

Stretch: Outside the Outcome 4 boundary but worth measuring for volume: Scheduling (Outcome 3): Can I get my appointment moved up? When can I schedule surgery? Why do I have to wait so long for surgery? Coverage and cost (Outcome 2): How much will this cost me? Will you get permission from my insurance?
Success measures
•	Share of answers rated safe and accurate, by cluster.
•	Clinician agreement with cluster, intent, and acuity tags (human versus ARCHIE).
•	Count of “must escalate” result and symptom questions identified.
FUTURE: 
•	Add resource documents (triage guidelines, education content) to the context.
•	Compare new AI outputs built with context layers (clinical data, education content, triage resources, real patient questions as context) against existing answers, using historical Q&A as the golden set. Requires a clinical validation layer for outputs.
•	Cross-reference and validate with MC Connect and CallMiner data.
5. Experiment 3: Gather
“What else should an agent ask when…?”
Main questions	Can an agent gather more information from the patient to determine whether it can answer the question?
When a clinician is needed, can the agent gather the right information for clinical decision making, so the clinician can act without messaging back and forth?
For the most common symptom and medication messages, what follow-up questions are needed, and what do secondary and tertiary branches look like?
What information is sufficient to answer, and what signals mean the agent must stop and hand off?
Goal	Learn which follow-up questions an agent should ask when a patient’s symptom or medication message (for example, “I’m having pain”) is incomplete. The questions serve two purposes: (a) determine whether the agent can safely answer, and (b) when it cannot, collect the information a clinician needs to make a decision, so the handoff arrives ready for action.
Value	Define the decision point between “agent answers” and “clinician takes over.”
Reduce repeated messaging by gathering the information clinicians typically need up front.
See where staff intuition and real patient messages agree or diverge.
Create evidence for guardrails, escalation thresholds, and handoff content (D5, G7).
Method
	Staff-led track	Data-led track
Step 1	Clinicians list common multi-part symptom and medication questions (1a).	Mine the in-basket for symptom and medication questions that needed follow-up, meaning more than one thread on a topic. Validate with staff (1b).
Step 2	Staff define the follow-up questions they would ask, and what they need to know to decide between answering and escalating (2a).	AI drafts follow-up questions and proposes what information is needed to answer versus hand off. Staff validate and compare against 2a (2b).

1.	Mine the in-basket for symptom and medication questions that needed follow-up, meaning more than one thread on a topic. Validate with staff (1b).
2.	AI drafts follow-up questions and proposes what information is needed to answer versus hand off. Staff validate and compare against 2a (2b).
3.	Use the combined staff and AI question set to drive a secondary query of the in-basket data. Check whether those follow-ups were actually asked in practice, and whether the answers patients gave would have let an agent (a) answer safely or (b) pass a complete, decision-ready summary to a clinician. Validate with staff.
Branching example
One day post-op, “I have a fever” leads to “Are you having pain or new redness?” “I have been vomiting” leads to “Have you been exposed to anyone with the flu?” Secondary and tertiary branches depend on the answers. For example, pain at the incision leads to infection questions, while pain in a toe does not.
Seed symptom and medication questions
Question	Clarifications an agent might need
I’m in so much pain and my appointment isn’t soon enough. What can I do right now?	Where is the pain? How severe? When did it start, and is it changing? What have you taken so far? Any weakness, numbness, or bowel or bladder changes?
The pain medication is making me sick to my stomach. What else can I take?	Which medication and dose? When did the nausea start relative to the dose? Nausea only, or vomiting? Can you keep fluids down? Any allergies or other medications?
Is this a normal reaction to my injection? (includes the rash at the injection site)	Which injection, and when? What does the site look like? Is it spreading, warm, draining, or itchy? Any fever?
Has my injection failed if I don’t have relief after 2 days?	Which injection and when? What was the pain level before and now? Is anything new or worse?
Should I be seen in the ED for this symptom?	What is the symptom? When did it start? How severe? Are any red-flag signs present?
Likely additional candidates: 
•	Should I take my medications the day of surgery? Which medications (blood thinners, diabetes medications, and so on)? What is the surgery date? Did the surgeon give specific instructions?
•	My dressing fell off. When was the surgery? Is there drainage, redness, or bleeding? Is the incision open?
•	My medication isn’t working. Which medication? How long have you taken it? What is the pain now compared with before?
•	Questions about the osteoporosis medication side effects. Which medication? Are you experiencing a symptom now, or asking in general?
•	Should I seek a second opinion for my pain issue? What is the pain issue, and what is the current plan?
•	What should I do if my PCP can’t provide the help I need? What help are you looking for, and what symptoms are you having?
•	Why haven’t I heard back from my provider? Which message, and when was it sent? Have your symptoms changed since then? The urgency check is the important one.
Success measures
•	Share of follow-up questions identified by both tracks.
•	Share of data-led follow-ups that staff validate.
•	Share of threads where a needed follow-up was asked in practice.
•	Share of question paths where the gathered information is enough to answer, versus requires handoff.
•	Clinician rating of whether the handoff information set is sufficient for a decision without further patient contact.
Stretch: Add triage guidelines to the context and test whether they improve AI-generated follow-up questions at either layer. Also identify secondary and tertiary question threads.
6. Suggested Sequencing
Day	Focus
Tuesday, Nov 17	Confirm data access. Lock cluster definitions and the tagging schema. Finalize question lists. Agree on success measures and thresholds. Finalize experiments and the minimum prototype flows.
Wednesday, Nov 18	Run Experiments 1 and 2. Begin clinician review.
Thursday, Nov 19	Run Experiment 3. Build the matrix. Run the human-versus-ARCHIE validation. Draft the readout and follow-on backlog.

Appendix A: Consolidated Clinical Question Bank
This bank combines the categorized clinical partner list with the questions from Jodell, MAA, Becky, and the APP team. Duplicates are merged, and related questions are grouped. The result is 45 consolidated entries across 12 categories.
What the consolidation shows
•	Strong convergence. Every question in Becky’s, Jodell’s, and MAA’s lists also appears in the categorized clinical list, so the sources agree. The pain-while-waiting question appears in three of them, a signal that it is likely high frequency.
•	Post-visit recall questions depend on patient context. Questions such as “what medication did the surgeon mention” cannot be answered from historical in-basket answers alone. They need visit notes, orders, and referrals, which tests the clinical context layer.
•	Some questions sit outside Outcome 4. Scheduling (Outcome 3) and cost and insurance (Outcome 2) are included for volume measurement only. The caregiver question is deferred.
•	Experiment 3 fit is concentrated. The injection reaction, injection failure, pain-while-waiting, medication nausea, and ED questions are the best candidates for information gathering and handoff.
Surgery overview and scheduling
Question	Experiment fit	Notes
Describe the surgery for me.	Exp 1, 2	Trigger: surgery discussed or scheduled.
How long is surgery?	Exp 1, 2	
When can I schedule surgery?	Exp 2 (volume)	Likely Outcome 3 (scheduling).
Why do I have to wait so long for surgery?	Exp 2 (volume)	Likely Outcome 3. Expectation-setting opportunity.
Can I get my appointment moved up?	Exp 2 (volume)	Likely Outcome 3. APP: patients with split medical and surgical visits often expect the medical team to move the surgical date.
Can my wife stay at the hospital during surgery? How will she get updates?	Not tested	Shared understanding (caregiver). Deliberately deferred.
